stion; add-only and mutating routes keep the read-only confidence floor.\n\n  Line 722:   return item.mutating === 'read-only';\n\n  Line 755:   const leadPhrases = Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(item, 'lead_phrases')\n\n  Line 756:     ? stringArray(item.lead_phrases, `${label}.lead_phrases`)\n\n  Line 760:     mutating: item.mutating,\n\n  Line 767:         `[command-bridges] ${label}.lead_phrases entry ${JSON.stringify(phrase)} must also appear in trigger_phrases`,\n\n  Line 772:         `[command-bridges] ${label}.lead_phrases entry ${JSON.stringify(phrase)} needs a state or health word because its route does not ask first`,\n\n  Line 1071:     lines.push(`        \"lead_phrases\": ${pythonList(runtime.leadPhrases)},`);\n\n  Line 1171:       ...(policyRecord.mutating ? { mutating: policyRecord.mutating } : {}),\n\n  Line 1206:       if (row.mutating) fields.push(`mutating: ${tsString(row.mutating)}`);\n\n  Line 1353:       ...(meaning ? { embeddingText: commandEmbeddingText(meaning) } : {}),\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md:\n  Line 4: trigger_phrases:\n\n  Line 50: The deriver also updates generated projection blocks in the live TypeScript and Python scoring sources. Its `GENERATED COMMAND ROWS` block in `lib/command-attach.ts` holds `COMMAND_ROWS`, one row per id a scorer can return for an enabled entry, covering the entry id and every enabled runtime id. Each row carries `command`, the runnable slash command, `policy`, its derived policy, `mutating`, the class that says what a run writes (`read-only`, `add-only` or `mutates`) when the record names one, and `ownerSkillId`, the owning skill. It writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` block into the same file, one row per hub mode that names a command, and fails when a mode names a command with no file under `.skilled/commands/`. The same block also holds `TRACKED_COMMANDS`, one row per tracked command keyed by its runnable string, emitted for every bridge entry whether or not the bridge is active. A surface that attaches a command reads its policy from `TRACKED_COMMANDS`. Each TypeScript bridge in `lib/scorer/projection.ts` carries an `ownerSkill`, the skill that owns its command. The generated JSON and generated source blocks are outputs, not hand-edited inputs.\n\n  Line 54: Each bridge row with TypeScript enabled also carries an `embeddingText`, the plain sentence a scoring vector is built from. A Python-only row carries none. A metadata row leads with its `userIntent.job` sentence and adds every `userIntent.ownedSignals` phrase, because both are written in the asker's voice. The entry `description` stands in for the job only when the record states no job. A doctor row builds it from the router frontmatter `description` and the `trigger_phrases` of the route. The deriver drops each slash command string before the parts are joined, because a name left in would hand the scorer a second copy of the token it already matches literally. The field is derived on every run and never authored, so edit the sources rather than the JSON.\n\n  Line 68: A doctor bridge passes on its own wording (an explicit-author phrase match). When none passes and no `/doctor:` command was typed, the scorer asks `/doctor:helper`'s own ranker, `doctor-helper-match.cjs`, whether the prompt describes a doctor problem. If it does, `command-doctor-helper` passes, and `/doctor:helper` then names the command. With no skill passing, a ranker score of 10 or more is enough, or 6 or more when the prompt names a problem. Beside a passing skill, the prompt must name a wrong state or a health check, such as `is not registered`, `never converges` or `turn off`. A `/doctor:` command counts as typed when the prompt starts with `/doctor:` or when it directly follows `run`, `execute`, `start`, `launch`, `use` or `kick off` outside quotes and backticks. A quoted command or a bare mid-sentence mention does not count. A typed command puts its own passing bridge first. A prompt that contains one of a bridge's `lead_phrases` also puts that passing bridge first, so `check my environment variables` leads with `/doctor:env`. For other prompts, a passing skill keeps first place and passing doctor bridges sit right after the second passing skill or the only passing skill. This keeps a doctor line within the CLI's three shown results. A typed command shows its matching runnable string on the top line. A skill-first brief shows `Also: /doctor:<command> <target>` on the second line. The Python fallback matches a doctor bridge only on a typed slash command.\n\n  Line 74: Every command bridge, not only a doctor bridge, passes on its own wording. `requireCommandPhraseAnchor` in `lib/scorer/fusion.ts` drops a passing command row below threshold unless the explicit-author lane matched one of its phrases or its slash string. The one other way in is meaning: a real embedder cosine of at least 0.72 against the row's `embeddingText` vector, with a 0.02 lead over the next command row. That needs the local embedder, so without it every command row passes on its wording alone. A prompt that asks for no change rules out every command on meaning, and a read-only question admits only a bridge marked `readOnly`.\n\n  Line 82: - TypeScript: the `userIntent.ownedSignals` phrases become the keywords and intent signals, the command's namespace and `command` become the domains, and a `read-only` `mutating` class sets `readOnly: true`.\n\n  Line 113: | `mutating` | `read-only`, `add-only`, `mutates` | Every metadata entry and every doctor route or action |\n\n  Line 124: | 3 | `mutating` is `read-only` | `run` |\n\n  Line 125: | 4 | `mutating` is `add-only` with `write_scope` set to `spec-folder` | `run` |\n\n  Line 128: `commandPolicies` in `command-bridges.generated.json` holds one record per command, or one record per route or action for a doctor command. Each record carries five keys: `command`, `route`, `mutating`, `askFirst`, `policy`. Nothing scores on it yet.\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/command-bridges.generated.json:\n  Line 24:       \"mutating\": \"mutates\",\n\n\n(Results are truncated: showing first 40 results. Consider using a more specific path or pattern.)","title":"grep","metadata":{"metadata":{"matches":40,"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Found 40 matches\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs:\n  Line 51: // silent fallthrough would hand a mutating command the read-only policy (or the\n\n  Line 183:         mutating: entry.mutating,\n\n  Line 282:     const phrases = stringArray(route.trigger_phrases, `${label}.trigger_phrases`);\n\n  Line 308:     const phrases = Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(item, 'trigger_phrases')\n\n  Line 309:       ? stringArray(item.trigger_phrases, `${label}.trigger_phrases`)\n\n  Line 347:     const phrases = stringArray(item.trigger_phrases, `${label}.trigger_phrases`);\n\n  Line 457:   if (!MUTATING_CLASSES.includes(record.mutating)) {\n\n  Line 459:       `[command-bridges] ${label} has mutating ${JSON.stringify(record.mutating)}; `\n\n  Line 492:   if (record.doctor === true && record.mutating === 'mutates') return 'ask';\n\n  Line 493:   if (record.mutating === 'read-only') return 'run';\n\n  Line 494:   if (record.mutating === 'add-only' && record.write_scope === 'spec-folder') return 'run';\n\n  Line 523:         mutating: entry.mutating,\n\n  Line 551:       mutating: fields.mutating,\n\n  Line 568:       mutating: route.mutating,\n\n  Line 582:       mutating: item?.mutating,\n\n  Line 603:           mutating: entry?.mutating,\n\n  Line 613:       mutating: item.mutating,\n\n  Line 654:       mutating: record.mutating,\n\n  Line 720: