{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794670040251,"sessionID":"ses_e255ff46effe7EWyuOLOEwVdy1","part":{"partID":"prt_1daab8e7b001GIY2Oj5kjaJlQk","sessionID":"ses_e255ff46effe7EWyuOLOEwVdy1","messageID":"msg_1daab6fde001MzFnBaXopDZPeW","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-bf5c6a1b90825795","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/state/state-reducer-registry.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/state/state-reducer-registry.md, lines 1-123\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Deep Review State Reducer And Registry\"\n3: description: \"Focused reference for reducer ownership, findings registry semantics, fail-closed behavior, and reconstruction rules.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"deep review reducer registry\"\n6:   - \"review findings registry\"\n7:   - \"deep review reducer ownership\"\n8:   - \"review state reconstruction\"\n9: importance_tier: important\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.11.1.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Deep Review State Reducer And Registry\n15: \n16: The reducer is the single writer for derived review state. Iteration agents append evidence; the reducer reconciles it into registry, dashboard, and strategy views.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Keep the findings registry, dashboard, and strategy current by deriving them from append-only evidence under the reducer, never as a second source of truth.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Deciding which writer owns a derived surface, or whether a hand edit is legitimate.\n29: - Recovering from corrupt JSONL, or rebuilding derived files that are missing.\n30: - Checking what a finding must carry before emission, and how duplicate findings collapse.\n31: \n32: ---\n33: \n34: ## 2. OWNERSHIP\n35: \n36: | Surface | Writer | Rule |\n37: |---------|--------|------|\n38: | JSONL state | Workflow plus validated iteration append | Append-only |\n39: | Findings registry | `scripts/reduce-state.cjs` | Regenerate from JSONL |\n40: | Dashboard | `scripts/reduce-state.cjs` | Regenerate from registry and latest events |\n41: | Strategy machine sections | `scripts/reduce-state.cjs` | Update from coverage and findings |\n42: | Iteration markdown | Review iteration agent | Write-once |\n43: | Review report | Synthesis | Final handoff |\n44: \n45: No agent should hand-edit reducer-owned derived state as the authoritative fix. Repair JSONL or rerun the reducer.\n46: \n47: Under ledger authority the JSONL the reducer reads is a projection of the ledger, bounded by the registered stems.\n48: \n49: - The registered vocabulary and the producer census are in `state-format.md` section 11.\n50: - `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/check-ledger-stem-producers.cjs` holds the census to the emitter surface on disk.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 3. FINDINGS REGISTRY\n55: \n56: The registry groups findings into:\n57: \n58: - Active findings.\n59: - Resolved findings.\n60: - Repeated findings.\n61: - Blocked or disputed findings.\n62: - Claim-adjudicated severity changes.\n63: \n64: Each active finding needs severity, category, file:line evidence, finding class, content hash, first-seen iteration, and latest-seen iteration.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 4. FINDING DEDUPLICATION\n69: \n70: The reducer deduplicates findings at synthesis time using a two-tier match.\n71: \n72: ### Two-Tier Match\n73: \n74: 1. **PRIMARY: content_hash** = `sha256(file_path + line_range + finding_type + normalized_description_80chars)`\n75:    - `file_path`: repo-relative path of the finding\n76:    - `line_range`: e.g., `\"42\"` or `\"42-56\"`\n77:    - `finding_type`: one of `security`, `correctness`, `performance`, `maintainability`, `test_quality`, `contract_safety`, `removal`\n78:    - `normalized_description_80chars`: first 80 characters of the description, whitespace-collapsed and lowercased\n79: 2. **FALLBACK (legacy records): file:line + normalized_title** - applied when one or both records lack a `content_hash`, preserving existing behavior unchanged.\n80: \n81: ### Synthesis Behavior\n82: \n83: When the same `content_hash` appears across iterations from different dimensions, synthesis collapses them to ONE entry with `dimensions: [<all dimensions that emitted it>]` rather than emitting multiple records. Records without `content_hash` fall back to `file:line + normalized_title`; no migration is required for existing JSONL state.\n84: \n85: ### Emission Requirement\n86: \n87: Every finding emitted into the JSONL delta (`findingDetails[]`) MUST include a `content_hash` field computed per the two-tier match. `reduce-state.cjs` reads this field for synthesis dedup.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 5. FAIL-CLOSED BEHAVIOR\n92: \n93: The reducer must refuse to publish derived state when JSONL is corrupt in strict mode. That protects dashboards and verdict previews from silently losing findings.\n94: \n95: Recovery order:\n96: \n97: 1. Identify the malformed JSONL line.\n98: 2. Repair or remove only the invalid appended record with an audit note.\n99: 3. Re-run the reducer.\n100: 4. Confirm registry and dashboard regenerate from the repaired log.\n101: \n102: ---\n103: \n104: ## 6. RECONSTRUCTION\n105: \n106: When derived files are missing but JSONL and iteration markdown exist:\n107: \n108: 1. Rebuild the registry from JSONL finding details.\n109: 2. Rebuild dashboard metrics from registry and latest convergence events.\n110: 3. Rebuild strategy from completed dimensions and active findings.\n111: 4. Re-run synthesis only after reducer outputs are consistent.\n112: \n113: If JSONL is incomplete but iteration files exist, recovery is manual and must be recorded before release readiness is claimed.\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n118: \n119: - `scripts/reduce-state.cjs` is the reducer implementation.\n120: - `references/state/state-format.md` defines state records.\n121: - `references/state/state-outputs.md` defines operator-visible outputs.\n122: - `references/convergence/convergence-signals.md` defines STOP fields reducer views display.\n123: ","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/state/state-reducer-registry.md, lines 1-123\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Deep Review State Reducer And Registry\"\n3: description: \"Focused reference for reducer ownership, findings registry semantics, fail-closed behavior, and reconstruction rules.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"deep review reducer registry\"\n6:   - \"review findings registry\"\n7:   - \"deep review reducer ownership\"\n8:   - \"review state reconstruction\"\n9: importance_tier: important\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.11.1.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Deep Review State Reducer And Registry\n15: \n16: The reducer is the single writer for derived review state. Iteration agents append evidence; the reducer reconciles it into registry, dashboard, and strategy views.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Keep the findings registry, dashboard, and strategy current by deriving them from append-only evidence under the reducer, never as a second source of truth.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Deciding which writer owns a derived surface, or whether a hand edit is legitimate.\n29: - Recovering from corrupt JSONL, or rebuilding derived files that are missing.\n30: - Checking what a finding must carry before emission, and how duplicate findings collapse.\n31: \n32: ---\n33: \n34: ## 2. OWNERSHIP\n35: \n36: | Surface | Writer | Rule |\n37: |---------|--------|------|\n38: | JSONL state | Workflow plus validated iteration append | Append-only |\n39: | Findings registry | `scripts/reduce-state.cjs` | Regenerate from JSONL |\n40: | Dashboard | `scripts/reduce-state.cjs` | Regenerate from registry and latest events |\n41: | Strategy machine sections | `scripts/reduce-state.cjs` | Update from coverage and findings |\n42: | Iteration markdown | Review iteration agent | Write-once |\n43: | Review report | Synthesis | Final handoff |\n44: \n45: No agent should hand-edit reducer-owned derived state as the authoritative fix. Repair JSONL or rerun the reducer.\n46: \n47: Under ledger authority the JSONL the reducer reads is a projection of the ledger, bounded by the registered stems.\n48: \n49: - The registered vocabulary and the producer census are in `state-format.md` section 11.\n50: - `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/check-ledger-stem-producers.cjs` holds the census to the emitter surface on disk.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 3. FINDINGS REGISTRY\n55: \n56: The registry groups findings into:\n57: \n58: - Active findings.\n59: - Resolved findings.\n60: - Repeated findings.\n61: - Blocked or disputed findings.\n62: - Claim-adjudicated severity changes.\n63: \n64: Each active finding needs severity, category, file:line evidence, finding class, content hash, first-seen iteration, and latest-seen iteration.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 4. FINDING DEDUPLICATION\n69: \n70: The reducer deduplicates findings at synthesis time using a two-tier match.\n71: \n72: ### Two-Tier Match\n73: \n74: 1. **PRIMARY: content_hash** = `sha256(file_path + line_range + finding_type + normalized_description_80chars)`\n75:    - `file_path`: repo-relative path of the finding\n76:    - `line_range`: e.g., `\"42\"` or `\"42-56\"`\n77:    - `finding_type`: one of `security`, `correctness`, `performance`, `maintainability`, `test_quality`, `contract_safety`, `removal`\n78:    - `normalized_description_80chars`: first 80 characters of the description, whitespace-collapsed and lowercased\n79: 2. **FALLBACK (legacy records): file:line + normalized_title** - applied when one or both records lack a `content_hash`, preserving existing behavior unchanged.\n80: \n81: ### Synthesis Behavior\n82: \n83: When the same `content_hash` appears across iterations from different dimensions, synthesis collapses them to ONE entry with `dimensions: [<all dimensions that emitted it>]` rather than emitting multiple records. Records without `content_hash` fall back to `file:line + normalized_title`; no migration is required for existing JSONL state.\n84: \n85: ### Emission Requirement\n86: \n87: Every finding emitted into the JSONL delta (`findingDetails[]`) MUST include a `content_hash` field computed per the two-tier match. `reduce-state.cjs` reads this field for synthesis dedup.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 5. FAIL-CLOSED BEHAVIOR\n92: \n93: The reducer must refuse to publish derived state when JSONL is corrupt in strict mode. That protects dashboards and verdict previews from silently losing findings.\n94: \n95: Recovery order:\n96: \n97: 1. Identify the malformed JSONL line.\n98: 2. Repair or remove only the invalid appended record with an audit note.\n99: 3. Re-run the reducer.\n100: 4. Confirm registry and dashboard regenerate from the repaired log.\n101: \n102: ---\n103: \n104: ## 6. RECONSTRUCTION\n105: \n106: When derived files are missing but JSONL and iteration markdown exist:\n107: \n108: 1. Rebuild the registry from JSONL finding details.\n109: 2. Rebuild dashboard metrics from registry and latest convergence events.\n110: 3. Rebuild strategy from completed dimensions and active findings.\n111: 4. Re-run synthesis only after reducer outputs are consistent.\n112: \n113: If JSONL is incomplete but iteration files exist, recovery is manual and must be recorded before release readiness is claimed.\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n118: \n119: - `scripts/reduce-state.cjs` is the reducer implementation.\n120: - `references/state/state-format.md` defines state records.\n121: - `references/state/state-outputs.md` defines operator-visible outputs.\n122: - `references/convergence/convergence-signals.md` defines STOP fields reducer views display.\n123: "}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \".skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/state/state-reducer-registry.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794670038651,"end":1794670040251}}}}
